  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Strategic Imaginations shows early scholarly uptake and notable reader interest: it has been cited once and downloaded 2,144 times through OAPEN. The book also aligns with the SDG area of Reduced inequalities, and its KU Leuven funding support indicates institutional backing for this research.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2020, Strategic Imaginations examines imaginations of female rule and the strategies women rulers used to negotiate political sovereignty. Its interdisciplinary framing connects gender studies, political thought, art history, and related humanities fields, giving the book a reach that fits both historical inquiry and contemporary debates about power, democracy, and representation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is readership rather than citation volume. OAPEN download data show sustained use, with 2,144 downloads recorded, suggesting the book has attracted attention from readers beyond its immediate authorship and publication context. Because download activity and citation uptake measure different forms of engagement, the record points to both access and emerging scholarly attention without collapsing them into a single metric.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly citation evidence is still early but present. OpenAlex records one citation, and the citing work appears in a humanities venue focused on public-facing scholarship, engaging themes of feminism, rhetoric, and right-wing populism. That pattern suggests the book’s arguments are resonating in current discussions of gendered political rhetoric and the historical framing of sovereignty.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book is also openly available in hybrid form, which likely supports its visibility and use, and the record notes KU Leuven as a funder. With one SDG alignment flagged under Reduced inequalities, the publication’s relevance extends beyond disciplinary scholarship into broader conversations about inequality and political inclusion. Overall, the evidence indicates a book with clear readership, early citation uptake, and a topic profile that remains timely in gender and political studies.</p>",
